<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herzegovina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herzegovina is the southern region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and Herzegovina, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neretva">Neretva</a> valley and the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mostar">Mostar</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it formed the principality of Hum (Zachlumia), and it takes its name from the title herceg, duke, used by the nobleman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stjepan-vukcic-kosaca">Stjepan Vukčić Kosača</a> in the 15th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> conquered it in the 1480s, and an uprising there in 1875 opened the Great Eastern Crisis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herzegovina.jpg" alt="Herzegovina" width="320" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lommes · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herzegovina_location_map_simple.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Herzegovina is the southern and smaller of the two main geographical regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and Herzegovina, and its borders have never been strictly defined. Its area is about 24 per cent of the country, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mostar">Mostar</a>, at its centre, is the largest city and unofficial capital. The terrain is mostly hilly karst, with high mountains such as Čvrsnica and Prenj in the north, except for the central valley of the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neretva">Neretva</a>, which flows past Konjic and Mostar to the Adriatic. The region has a coastline of about 20 km. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stolac">Stolac</a> is perhaps its oldest city, with settlement from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a> and the Illyrian town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daorson">Daorson</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> in the 7th century, and the area later called Herzegovina was divided in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, Zachlumia and Travunia. Before the name Herzegovina spread, it was called Hum or Humska zemlja. The Bosnian ban Stjepan Kotromanić annexed Hum in 1326, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a>, arriving in the 1340s, began to spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in a population that was overwhelmingly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>. King Tvrtko I gave parts of Hum to Vlatko Vuković of the Kosača family. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stjepan-vukcic-kosaca">Stjepan Vukčić Kosača</a>, the last Bosnian nobleman to hold effective control of the province, took the Slavic title herceg, duke, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, who named conquered lands after their earlier rulers, called the region Herzegovina.</p><p>Stjepan died in 1466, and his son Vlatko Hercegović resisted until December 1481, when he agreed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> to move with his family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. The region was organised as the Sanjak of Herzegovina with its seat at Foča. Serbs rose in 1596–97 and were defeated at Gacko. The Herzegovina Uprising of 1875–78 was backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and Montenegro. By the Treaty of Berlin of 1878 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> occupied Herzegovina with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and annexed them in 1908, which brought on the Bosnian Crisis. In 1918 the region joined the Kingdom of Serbs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Herzegovina is divided between the two entities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and Herzegovina, Republika Srpska and the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina. Its cultural monuments include Mogorjelo, the Stari most at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mostar">Mostar</a> and the stećci, the large medieval stone grave monuments. Natural landmarks include the Kravica waterfalls on the Trebižat river, the Hutovo Blato bird reserve and the Vjetrenica cave system.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herzegovina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Efforts in 1991 to bring the Socialist Republic of Bosnia and <b>Herzegovina</b> into the federation, through talks between Milošević, Bosnia's Serbian Democratic Party and the Bosniak vice-president Adil Zulfikarpašić, ended by late 1991, when Izetbegović planned a referendum on independence and violence broke out between Serbs and Bosniaks.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2016 about 700 troops were stationed abroad: 100 with the NATO-led ISAF force in Afghanistan, 210 in Kosovo under KFOR and 160 in Bosnia and <b>Herzegovina</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The NDH covered most of present-day Croatia, all of Bosnia and <b>Herzegovina</b>, part of Serbia and a small part of Slovenia, while Italy and Hungary took Dalmatia, the islands, Međimurje and southern Baranja.</blockquote><a href="/empire/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its annexation of Bosnia and <b>Herzegovina</b> in October 1908, where Serbia had hoped to expand, set off the Bosnian Crisis of 1908–1909, which permanently damaged relations between Austria-Hungary and Russia and Serbia and was among the causes of World War I. The Treaty of London of May 1913…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The six republics, Bosnia and <b>Herzegovina</b>, Croatia, Macedonia, Montenegro, Serbia and Slovenia, were constitutionally equal; Serbia alone contained two autonomous provinces, Kosovo and Vojvodina, which the 1974 constitution gave voting power equal to the republics', to the lasting anger of Serbian officials.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Military service was required of non-Muslims in 1856, but exemptions could be bought, angering poorer Christians, and tax grievances helped spark the <b>Herzegovina</b> Uprising of 1875.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
